        <Literal xml:lang="en">standardized mean difference is statistic computed by forming the difference between two means, divided by an estimate of the within-group standard deviation.
It is used to provide an estimation of the effect size between two treatments when the predictor (independent variable) is categorical and the response(dependent) variable is continuous.

A standardized mean difference is a statistic that is a difference between two means, divided by a statistical measure of dispersion. 

The term Standardized Mean Difference is a description of the concept without an explicit type of statistical measure of dispersion. If the statistical measure of dispersion is specified, then a type (child term) of Standardized Mean Difference is preferred.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
