        <Literal xml:lang="en">one sided test</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000286</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">adapted from:
http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/mult_pkg/faq/general/tail_tests.htm</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000041"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000286</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en"></Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000286</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">one tailed test</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000287</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">For example, we may wish to compare the mean of a sample to a given value x using a t-test.  Our null hypothesis is that the mean is equal to x. A two-tailed test will test both if the mean is significantly greater than x and if the mean significantly less than x. The mean is considered significantly different from x if the test statistic is in the top 2.5% or bottom 2.5% of its probability distribution, resulting in a p-value less than 0.05.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000114"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000287</IRI>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000122</IRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000287</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">a two tailed test is a statistical test which assess the null hypothesis of absence of difference assuming a symmetric (not skewed) underlying probability distribution by allocating half of the significance level selected to each of the direction of change which could explain a difference (for example, a difference can be an excess or a loss).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000116"/>
        <IRI>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/STATO_0000287</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Added following a term request by Chris Mungall:
https://github.com/ISA-tools/stato/issues/6</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
